T35.01|Utility represents how a person values outcomes rather than assuming each additional rupee has equal personal value. Risk preferences describe choices among uncertain outcomes; diminishing marginal utility can make a certain amount preferable to a gamble with the same expected wealth. Circumstances affect preferences and capacity.~~Expected value and preferred choice are therefore different concepts. A choice is not irrational simply because it avoids a positive expected monetary gamble.|Risk aversion: Prefers less uncertainty in comparable outcomes.;;Risk neutrality: Focuses on expected value under the model.;;Risk seeking: Prefers greater uncertainty in comparable outcomes.|Choose between certain ₹1 lakh and a 50/50 chance of ₹0 or ₹2 lakh.~~Both have ₹1 lakh expected monetary value.;;Compare their utility and consequences for essential needs.;;Separate preference from financial ability to absorb loss.~~Equal expected money does not imply equal desirability.~~Utility is a model of preference, not a universal formula.|Identify the relevant outcomes.;;Separate expected value from utility.;;Consider constraints and loss capacity.
T35.02|Prospect theory describes choices influenced by reference points, loss aversion and probability weighting. A loss relative to a perceived benchmark can feel more consequential than an equal gain. This can influence investment decisions even when future economics are unchanged.~~The pattern is descriptive and context-dependent. Recognising it helps examine whether a past purchase price is distorting a present decision.|Reference dependence: Outcomes assessed relative to a benchmark.;;Loss aversion: Losses receive stronger weight than comparable gains.;;Probability weighting: Perceived impact differs from objective probability.|An investor bought at ₹100; the share is now ₹70 and new evidence supports value ₹60.~~Ignore the desire to return to the purchase price temporarily.;;Evaluate future cash flows and alternatives from today's position.;;Include taxes and costs explicitly.~~The original price does not establish current value.~~Selling is not automatically optimal; the current evidence must support it.|Identify the reference point.;;Reassess forward economics.;;Avoid equating loss realisation with new loss creation.
T35.03|Anchoring gives excessive influence to an initial number; framing changes choices through presentation of equivalent facts. Financial forecasts can inherit a management target or recent price without adequate independent analysis. Percentages and absolute amounts can create different impressions of the same exposure.~~Use multiple benchmarks and restate alternatives in comparable units. An anchor can be useful evidence, but should not silently determine the answer.|Price anchor: Past or quoted value dominates analysis.;;Forecast anchor: Initial estimate constrains later revisions.;;Framing: Presentation changes perceived trade-offs.|A fee is presented as only 1% on ₹1 crore assets.~~Convert it to ₹1 lakh per year at that balance.;;Compare services and all other charges.;;Evaluate the same fee in both percentage and cash terms.~~Reframing reveals its economic size.~~Value depends on service and alternatives, not wording alone.|Use independent estimates.;;Convert percentages to amounts.;;Restate choices on consistent horizons.
T35.04|Mental accounting treats money differently depending on its source or label. Separate budgets can improve discipline, but can also conceal that resources and risks belong to one household balance sheet. A windfall is still part of total wealth.~~Evaluate borrowing and investing together. Keeping costly debt while holding low-return cash may or may not be justified by liquidity, but the trade-off should be explicit.|Source account: Salary, bonus or windfall labels.;;Purpose account: Funds earmarked for goals.;;Portfolio account: Evaluates holdings separately from total wealth.|A household keeps ₹1 lakh earning assumed 4% while owing ₹1 lakh at 15%.~~Annual interest received is ₹4,000.;;Interest paid is ₹15,000.;;Compare the ₹11,000 gap with genuine liquidity needs and repayment terms.~~Separate labels can obscure the combined cost.~~Tax and emergency access may affect the decision.|Review the whole balance sheet.;;Use earmarking deliberately.;;Make liquidity and interest trade-offs explicit.
T35.05|Overconfidence overstates one's knowledge, forecasting precision or control. Self-attribution credits successes to skill while explaining failures through external causes. Together they can increase trading, concentration and resistance to contrary evidence.~~Distinguish decision quality from realised outcome. A sound process can lose through uncertainty, while a weak process can win by luck.|Calibration error: Confidence exceeds accuracy.;;Control illusion: Assumes influence over random outcomes.;;Self-attribution: Interprets wins and losses asymmetrically.|An analyst assigns 90% confidence to ten predictions but only six occur.~~Compare the six successes with the expected calibration.;;Inspect sample size and prediction definitions.;;Review both wins and losses using the same criteria.~~Confidence needs evidence from repeated forecasting.~~Ten observations alone give limited statistical precision.|Record probabilities beforehand.;;Review outcomes consistently.;;Limit concentration unsupported by evidence.
T35.06|Confirmation bias favours evidence supporting an existing belief; availability bias overweights memorable or easily recalled events. Both can distort research selection and probability estimates. Recent media attention is not a representative sample of outcomes.~~Seek evidence that could disprove the thesis and use base rates from appropriate populations. Distinguish absence of evidence from evidence of absence.|Confirmation: Selective interpretation and search.;;Availability: Salient examples dominate judgment.;;Base-rate neglect: Ignores broader frequencies.|An investor studies three successful startups in news stories and concludes most startups succeed.~~Identify the selection process.;;Seek a complete, comparable cohort including failures.;;Compare success definitions and time horizons.~~Visible winners cannot establish the population success rate.~~Good base rates still need case-specific adjustment.|Search for disconfirming facts.;;Use representative samples.;;Separate media salience from frequency.
T35.07|Herding follows other participants rather than independent analysis; social proof treats popularity as evidence of correctness. Shared positions can create correlated behaviour and crowded exits. Following others may sometimes convey information, but it also transmits errors.~~Ask whether the crowd's reasons are independent or all trace back to the same claim. Price momentum alone does not establish intrinsic value.|Informational herd: Infers others know something.;;Reputational herd: Follows consensus to avoid standing out.;;Crowded positioning: Many holders depend on the same exit.|A share rises after one rumour repeated by hundreds of accounts.~~Trace the original source.;;Evaluate cash-flow evidence independently.;;Assess liquidity if holders try to exit together.~~Many repetitions are not many independent confirmations.~~Crowd behaviour can persist even when evidence is weak.|Trace information provenance.;;Develop independent valuation.;;Stress crowd exits and financing.
T35.08|Recency bias overweights recent outcomes; extrapolation extends observed trends without adequate causal support. A strong recent market can inflate return assumptions, while a recent crisis can make every future scenario look catastrophic. Regime changes limit simple trend continuation.~~Use longer histories, mechanisms and scenarios. Long data sets still require attention to structural changes rather than blind averaging.|Recency: Latest observations dominate.;;Trend extrapolation: Assumes current trajectory continues.;;Regime neglect: Ignores changing economic relationships.|Sales grew 30% in a rebound year from ₹100 to ₹130 lakh.~~A repeated 30% assumption gives ₹169 next year.;;Check whether the rebound was one-time.;;Build capacity and demand-based alternatives.~~One high-growth year does not justify permanent acceleration.~~A sustainable growth forecast needs operating evidence.|Separate rebound from trend.;;Use causal drivers.;;Test slower growth and changing regimes.
T35.09|The disposition effect describes a tendency to realise gains readily while retaining losing investments. This may reflect reference dependence, regret or a desire to avoid acknowledging mistakes. Tax and rebalancing motives can also explain sales, so behaviour needs context.~~Evaluate future expected return and portfolio fit, not merely whether a holding is above or below purchase price.|Gain realisation: Sells winners to secure satisfaction.;;Loss avoidance: Retains losers to avoid recognition.;;Forward review: Reassesses based on current evidence.|Two holdings cost ₹100 each; one trades at ₹120 with improving fundamentals, the other ₹70 with deteriorating fundamentals.~~Review both from current prices.;;Assess valuation, portfolio role and tax costs.;;Avoid selecting the sale solely by past gain or loss.~~Purchase history is not a complete investment thesis.~~The example does not prescribe which security to sell.|Reassess future economics.;;Review winners and losers equally.;;Document tax and portfolio reasons.
T35.10|Present bias gives immediate rewards excessive weight relative to future costs. Self-control mechanisms such as automatic saving, commitment rules and spending delays can help align actions with long-term goals. They should remain flexible enough for genuine emergencies.~~A financial plan must fit actual behaviour. An ambitious contribution schedule repeatedly abandoned may be less useful than a sustainable one.|Present bias: Favours immediate benefits.;;Commitment device: Prearranged rule supporting future goals.;;Automation: Reduces repeated discretionary decisions.|A saver plans ₹10,000 monthly but repeatedly spends it before month-end.~~Test an automatic transfer near payday.;;Maintain appropriate emergency access.;;Review whether the amount is sustainable.~~Designing the process can improve execution.~~Automation does not make an unsuitable investment appropriate.|Use realistic contributions.;;Automate suitable actions.;;Review emergency flexibility and behaviour.
T35.11|Bubbles and panics involve reinforcing expectations, financing and trading behaviour, but identifying them in real time is difficult. Rising collateral values can support leverage, while falling prices trigger forced sales. Narrative enthusiasm and liquidity conditions can interact with genuine innovation.~~An extreme valuation is evidence to examine, not a precise timing signal. Shorting an apparently overvalued asset can still create large interim losses.|Boom feedback: Price gains attract funding and demand.;;Panic feedback: Losses prompt withdrawals and sales.;;Fundamental uncertainty: Genuine future value remains disputed.|An asset rises from ₹100 to ₹200 and lenders increase permitted borrowing against it.~~Track leverage and collateral dependence.;;Stress a price decline and margin calls.;;Compare prices with supported cash-flow scenarios.~~Funding can amplify both ascent and reversal.~~No model reliably dates every turning point.|Separate fundamentals and financing.;;Stress leveraged exits.;;Avoid claiming precise bubble timing.
T35.12|Behavioural corporate finance studies how managerial and investor biases affect investment, acquisitions and financing. Optimistic managers may underestimate costs or overpay; market sentiment may influence issuance decisions. Governance and independent challenge can improve choices.~~Not every failed project proves bias. Compare forecasts, available evidence and decision procedures at the time.|Manager optimism: Overstates favourable business outcomes.;;Escalation: Continues a project because of sunk spending.;;Market timing: Financing choices respond to perceived mispricing.|A project has spent ₹20 lakh; completing it requires ₹30 and generates supported value ₹25.~~Treat the ₹20 as sunk for the incremental decision.;;Compare remaining cost ₹30 with benefit ₹25.;;Assess shutdown costs and strategic effects separately.~~Past spending does not make further spending valuable.~~Other genuine incremental benefits could change the decision.|Separate sunk and future costs.;;Use independent forecasts.;;Review incentives and stopping criteria.
T35.13|Decision journals record assumptions and reasons before outcomes are known. Checklists ensure important questions are addressed; pre-mortems imagine failure and investigate plausible causes before commitment. These tools improve review by preserving the original information set.~~They should encourage critical thinking rather than become ceremonial boxes. Record what would change the decision and when it will be reviewed.|Journal: Dated thesis, probabilities and evidence.;;Checklist: Required analytical and control steps.;;Pre-mortem: Explores plausible failure paths.|Before investing, a team records sales growth 10%, margin 15% and a review trigger if churn doubles.~~Identify supporting evidence and contrary cases.;;Set a review date and trigger.;;Compare later outcomes with the original assumptions.~~Written expectations reduce hindsight reconstruction.~~A checklist cannot eliminate uncertainty.|Write assumptions before acting.;;Define falsification and review triggers.;;Evaluate process as well as outcome.
T35.14|Incentives influence what people measure, disclose and optimise. Decision quality improves when rewards reflect durable value and appropriate risk rather than one convenient short-term metric. A measure can become misleading when people alter behaviour to maximise it.~~Consider gaming, delayed consequences and accountability. Rewarding volume without credit quality can create future losses outside the bonus period.|Metric incentive: Behaviour responds to measured targets.;;Horizon incentive: Rewards arrive before risks mature.;;Accountability: Decision makers bear or explain consequences.|A lender pays bonuses for ₹100 lakh new loans without considering defaults; ₹20 later becomes unrecoverable.~~Evaluate loss-adjusted economics.;;Add suitable quality and delayed-performance measures.;;Review whether incentives encourage concealment or excess risk.~~Production volume alone does not establish value.~~Controls and fair performance attribution are also needed.|Identify what rewards optimise.;;Include risk and time horizon.;;Test gaming and unintended effects.
